# VLA (ConvDelta)

**Release {{VERSION}}, step {{STEP}}** ({{TOKENS}} training tokens).

A language model with a fixed-size state and no positional encoding, built from convolutions on a 7 × 7 grid. Its
input is the last 8 tokens, placed on the grid by a learned map. Six residual ConvDelta blocks follow. Each one
is a causal time conv over the last 4 ticks, three 3 × 3 conv + BatchNorm + ReLU layers, and, at every grid
position, 6 heads of a 32 × 32 associative memory written by the gated delta rule and read by a query. Every
tick the model reads one token, updates its memories, and decodes the next token. The state is 2.1 M values and
never grows.

It is the text-trained core of a vision-language-action design. Most commercial applications for autonomous,
agile robots do not require superintelligence; they need conversational common sense, grounded in and modulated by
what the device perceives (sound, video, touch). To achieve superhuman hand-eye coordination, they need it at frame
rates up to 500 Hz, with low and deterministic latency for embedded control, on a power budget a battery can
supply. So the target is a single chip with no external DRAM: a CNN is parameter-efficient enough that every weight
fits in on-chip SRAM, where the weights stay programmable, and the fixed-size state makes every tick the same work.
Leaving out external memory also saves power: no DRAM chips to keep refreshed, no high-speed interface driving
signals between chips, and a weight read from on-chip SRAM takes on the order of a hundred times less energy than
one fetched from off-chip DRAM.

Code, functional spec and figures: [github.com/siliconperception/vla](https://github.com/siliconperception/vla)

![One clock tick](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/siliconperception/vla/main/docs/figures/vla_v540.svg)

## Use

```bash
git clone https://github.com/siliconperception/vla && cd vla && pip install -r requirements.txt
for p in "The colors of the rainbow are" \
         "The best way to learn a new language is" \
         "My grandmother's recipe for apple pie starts with" \
         "When you plant a vegetable garden, the first thing to do is" \
         "The history of the bicycle began in"; do
  python vla.py --pretrained --generate --n 120 --prompt "$p" --device cuda
done
```

`--pretrained` downloads `model.safetensors` and `vla_args.json` (the settings the weights were trained with)
and restores both. The weights are not a `transformers` model; load them through `vla.py`. This repository
holds only the current release: each new one replaces the weights and this card, and earlier releases are not
kept.

## Model

| | |
|---|---|
| Parameters | 55.7 M: 30.0 M trained, 25.8 M frozen (token embedding and output layer from pythia-31m) |
| State | 2,145,073 values, fixed in size: per block 49 positions × 6 heads × 32 × 32 memories and a 3-tick FIFO, plus the last 7 token ids |
| Input | the last 8 tokens, each embedded and mixed 1×1 (256 → 384), placed on the 7 × 7 grid by a learned map (starting as concentric rings, newest outside), then a learned gain and bias per position |
| Blocks | 6 × ConvDelta at 384 channels: causal depthwise time conv (4 ticks), 3 × (3×3 conv, BatchNorm, ReLU), 1×1 q, k, v, β, α, gated delta-rule memory per position and head, 1×1 out, residual adds |
| Memory | M ← α M (I − β k kᵀ) + β v kᵀ, read M q; q, k unit length; β, α computed from the input every tick; base decay windows 4 to 4,096 ticks |
| Readout | 3×3 projection (384 → 512), a tree of three 3×3 convs (7 → 5 → 3 → 1), the frozen pythia-31m output layer |
| Tokenizer | pythia-31m BPE (50,304) plus a `<NUL>` token ("say nothing this tick") |
| Compute | about 1.4 G multiply-accumulates per tick, the same every tick |

## Training

- From scratch, in one configuration: {{STEP}} steps × 32 rows, one token per row per step ({{TOKENS}} tokens).
  Each row trains on 256-tick windows, all ticks of a window at once (the memories through an exact chunked
  parallel scan); the state carries on between windows and the gradient stops at a window's start.
- AdamW (β 0.9, 0.95, no weight decay), learning rate warmed from 1e-5 to 0.001 over the first 256,000 steps,
  then held. The loss is normalized per word rather than per token.
- Data: FineWeb sample-10BT, text only. In training, a document start resets the state only half the time; in
  the other half the previous document's memories carry on, and the model must learn to drop them itself
  (a stress test for discontinuities in a robot's continuous perception). Evaluation resets at every document.
- The [spec](https://github.com/siliconperception/vla/blob/main/docs/spec/README.md) gives the exact computation.

## Evaluation (at the published step, {{STEP}})

The reference is EleutherAI's [pythia-70m](https://huggingface.co/EleutherAI/pythia-70m): a transformer of a
similar total size (70 M parameters, 19 M of them outside the token embedding and output layer) with the same
tokenizer, so losses per token compare directly. Pythia publishes checkpoints from early in training, so it is
shown at about this model's training tokens (steps 4 and 8 bracket it) as well as fully trained. Every model is
scored on the same documents and pairs, by the same rule.

| | **this model** | pythia-70m | pythia-70m | pythia-70m |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| training tokens | **{{TOKENS}}** | 8.4 M (step 4) | 16.8 M (step 8) | 300 B (final) |
| FineWeb held-out (100 documents, nats/token) | **{{FW}}** | {{PSTEP4_FW}} | {{PSTEP8_FW}} | {{PMAIN_FW}} |
| BabyLM 2026 dev (100 pieces, nats/token) | **{{BABY}}** | {{PSTEP4_BABY}} | {{PSTEP8_BABY}} | {{PMAIN_BABY}} |
| BLiMP (1,005 pairs from all 67 paradigms), % | **{{BLIMP}}** | {{PSTEP4_BLIMP}} | {{PSTEP8_BLIMP}} | {{PMAIN_BLIMP}} |
| ComVE (999 pairs, common sense), % | **{{COMVE}}** | {{PSTEP4_COMVE}} | {{PSTEP8_COMVE}} | {{PMAIN_COMVE}} |

Losses are the mean over documents of each document's mean loss per token, from a zero state (this model) or a
bare document start (pythia); a document with any token over 20 nats is counted as a runaway and left out
({{RUNAWAY}} of the FineWeb documents for this model). Pythia was trained on the Pile, this model on FineWeb, so
the FineWeb row is in-domain for this model only. Matched tokens are not matched training: pythia's 8.4 M and
16.8 M tokens are only 4 and 8 optimizer updates of 2 M tokens each, at the very start of its learning-rate
warm-up, while this model's are {{STEP}} steps of 32 tokens (1,562 updates). Fully trained pythia-70m is the fair
reference, and this model is far short of it. A pair is right when the model gives the correct sentence the lower
total loss, each sentence read without context; chance is 50 %. For scale on the pairs: babyllama-10m-2024
(trained on 10 M words) scores 70.0 / 63.7 and SmolLM2-135M 80.9 / 79.3.

